cone and cuts stalled CoreMark cycles by 6.1%, but the remaining register-file-to-ALU path limits median Fmax to 63.090 MHz, so fetch lookahead loses to preserving ID/EX operand flops.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:14:- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r7s1 (improvement, +11.64%): Registering complete ID/EX operands and \n…[9002 of 12074 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5bc3b16109ce338f]…\nhe prototype.\\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r12s1 (regression, -22.57%): Moving eight-bit carry-select additions before ID/EX puts arithmetic after MEM load extraction and forwarding on the data-memory return path, dropping median Gowin Fmax from 95.961 to 74.304 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles despite earlier RF snapshots.\\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r12s2 (regression, -8.27%): Four indexed 32-row BTB banks with XOR hashing and taken-only allocation cut CoreMark cycles by 3.09%, but exposed a fetch-PC-to-next-PC critical path that lowered median Gowin Fmax from 95.961 to 85.308 MHz, outweighing the conflict reduction.\\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r13s2 (regression, -1.90%): Deriving JAL/branch targets from instruction immediates with a 512-entry direction RAM cut CoreMark cycles by 3.32%, but dmemReady-to-ID provenance remained critical and median Gowin Fmax fell from 95.961 to 91.007 MHz, so avoiding wide target lookup still requires preserving backend timing.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
