{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot = Path.cwd()\npaths = [Path('/AGENTS.md'), *[p / 'AGENTS.md' for p in reversed(root.parents) if p \"'!= Path('\"'/')], root / 'AGENTS.md', root / 'cores/AGENTS.md', root / 'cores/bench/AGENTS.md']\nfor p in paths:\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'{p}:\\\\n{p.read_text()}')\np = root / 'cores/bench/LESSONS.md'\nprint(f'{p}:\\\\n{p.read_text()}')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_1-sol_xhigh-v2-rep5/cores/bench/LESSONS.md:\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r1s0 (regression, +17.57%): Sharing DIV/REM reduced Gowin logic use by 60%, but 32 serial sign-dependent 33-bit additions retained a deep critical path, limiting median Fmax gains to about 18% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +712.62%): A shared registered 32-step DIV/REM divider removes the deep combinational EX cone, allowing Fmax gains to outweigh divide stalls when CoreMark divisions occur mainly during initialization outside the timed loop.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.32%): A 32-entry tagged BTB cut CoreMark cycles by 6.3%, but forwarding through the combinational M-extension ALU still limited median Fmax to 5.58 MHz, so removing redirect bubbles delivered much less throughput than isolating the divider in a registered multicycle unit.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (regression, -8.68%): Registering branch recovery in EX/MEM adds a bubble per taken transfer (~8% more CoreMark cycles), while the regfile-to-ALU-to-dmem path limits median Fmax to 43.624 MHz, so cutting the redirect cone alone does not repay the added latency.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (improvement, +15.01%): A 32-entry tagged predictor improves throughput on the multicycle-divider core by removing correctly predicted transfer bubbles while ID-registered branch/JAL target matches and