{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r1s0 (regression, +17.57%): Sharing DIV/REM reduced Gowin logic use by 60%, but 32 serial sign-dependent 33-bit additions retained a deep critical path, limiting median Fmax gains to about 18% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +712.62%): A shared registered 32-step DIV/REM divider removes the deep combinational EX cone, allowing Fmax gains to outweigh divide stalls when CoreMark divisions occur mainly during initialization outside the timed loop.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.32%): A 32-entry tagged BTB cut CoreMark cycles by 6.3%, but forwarding through the combinational M-extension ALU still limited median Fmax to 5.58 MHz, so removing redirect bubbles delivered much less throughput than isolating the divider in a registered multicycle unit.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (regression, -8.68%): Registering branch recovery in EX/MEM adds a bubble per taken transfer (~8% more CoreMark cycles), while the regfile-to-ALU-to-dmem path limits median Fmax to 43.624 MHz, so cutting the redirect cone alone does not repay the added latency.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (improvement, +15.01%): A 32-entry tagged predictor improves throughput on the multicycle-divider core by removing correctly predicted transfer bubbles while ID-registered branch/JAL target matches and raw fetch bits with separate validity avoid serial target comparison and redirect-driven register-address muxes in EX recovery.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r3s1 (improvement, +16.27%): Registering both MUL partial products and JALR targets cuts competing EX timing cones, while preserving fast conditional-branch/JAL resolution lets Fmax gains outweigh completion stalls on CoreMark's relatively infrequent MUL/JALR instructions.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 (regression, +6.91%): A compact four-entry flow-through fetch FIFO overlaps load-use/dmem/divider holds with instruction-bus acceptance, but its gain lost to registering MUL/JALR (+16.27%), showi\n…[4175 of 7247 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:547e54c610b3b2f7]…\noming the timing cost that defeated flow-through queues.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r11s2 (regression, +5.75%): Separating BTB tag probing from constant-index direction/strength updates removes the predictor-maintenance cone while preserving indexed fetch lookup, but exposes RF/operand-capture and ALU/dmem timing limits that cap throughput gains despite only ten added CoreMark cycles in the prototype.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r12s1 (regression, -22.57%): Moving eight-bit carry-select additions before ID/EX puts arithmetic after MEM load extraction and forwarding on the data-memory return path, dropping median Gowin Fmax from 95.961 to 74.304 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles despite earlier RF snapshots.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r12s2 (regression, -8.27%): Four indexed 32-row BTB banks with XOR hashing and taken-only allocation cut CoreMark cycles by 3.09%, but exposed a fetch-PC-to-next-PC critical path that lowered median Gowin Fmax from 95.961 to 85.308 MHz, outweighing the conflict reduction.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
