{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r1s0 (regression, +17.57%): Sharing DIV/REM reduced Gowin logic use by 60%, but 32 serial sign-dependent 33-bit additions retained a deep critical path, limiting median Fmax gains to about 18% with unchanged CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +712.62%): A shared registered 32-step DIV/REM divider removes the deep combinational EX cone, allowing Fmax gains to outweigh divide stalls when CoreMark divisions occur mainly during initialization outside the timed loop.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.32%): A 32-entry tagged BTB cut CoreMark cycles by 6.3%, but forwarding through the combinational M-extension ALU still limited median Fmax to 5.58 MHz, so removing redirect bubbles delivered much less throughput than isolating the divider in a registered multicycle unit.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r2s1 (regression, -8.68%): Registering branch recovery in EX/MEM adds a bubble per taken transfer (~8% more CoreMark cycles), while the regfile-to-ALU-to-dmem path limits median Fmax to 43.624 MHz, so cutting the redirect cone alone does not repay the added latency.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r2s2 (improvement, +15.01%): A 32-entry tagged predictor improves throughput on the multicycle-divider core by removing correctly predicted transfer bubbles while ID-registered branch/JAL target matches and raw fetch bits with separate validity avoid serial target comparison and redirect-driven register-address muxes in EX recovery.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r3s1 (improvement, +16.27%): Registering both MUL partial products and JALR targets cuts competing EX timing cones, while preserving fast conditional-branch/JAL resolution lets Fmax gains outweigh completion stalls on CoreMark's relatively infrequent MUL/JALR instructions.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r3s2 (regression, +6.91%): A compact four-entry flow-through fetch FIFO overlaps load-use/dmem/divider holds with instruction-bus acceptance, but its gain lost to registering MUL/JALR (+16.27%), showing that fetch stall reduction can remain secondary to cutting EX timing cones.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r4s1 (improvement, +9.98%): Registering one-hot forwarding provenance at ID/EX removes destination-tag matching and bypass priority from the EX-to-ALU path without adding CoreMark cycles, with next-state producer matching and held-operand refresh required to preserve dependencies as bypass producers drain.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r5s0 (regression, +5.48%): Register-file primitive changes expose the late load/ALU WB mux, so pairing written-bit-masked distributed RAM with final-data selection before MEM/WB removes competing operand-delivery delays and shifts the critical path to forwarding controls.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r5s1 (improvement, +10.55%): Separately generated register-file word flops with balanced one-hot AND/OR read trees prevent Gowin from absorbing ID/EX operand flops into RAM read ports, removing RAM clock-to-output delay from the EX critical path at unchanged CoreMark cycles but increased flop/LUT area.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
