{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -u - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,subprocess\nfrom tools.eval.fpga import validate_coremark_uart\nfrom tools.eval.cosim import run_one\nroot=Path.cwd()\nprobe=root/'cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/r11_rf_control'\nsim=str(probe/'obj_dir/cosim_sim')\nelf=probe/'bench/coremark.elf'\nprint('Running canonical stalled CoreMark on RF-control prototype',flush=True)\nr=subprocess.run([sim,str(elf),'50000000','--bench','--istall','--dstall'],capture_output=True,text=True,timeout=180)\n(probe/'coremark.stdout').write_text(r.stdout)\n(probe/'coremark.stderr').write_text(r.stderr)\nassert r.returncode==0,(r.returncode,r.stdout[-1000:],r.stderr[-1000:])\nmarker=json.loads(r.stdout.splitlines()[-1])\nvalid,reason=validate_coremark_uart(marker.get('uart',''),10)\nassert valid,reason\nassert marker['ebreak'] and not marker['oob']\nassert marker['bench_complete']\ncycles=marker['bench_stop_cycle']-marker['bench_start_cycle']\nprint('CoreMark cycles',cycles,'CRC/UART validation passed',flush=True)\nprint('Projected fitness',95.075e6*10/cycles,'iter/sec',flush=True)\nsummary={'cycles':cycles,'fmax_mhz':95.075,'fitness':95.075e6*10/cycles,'coremark_valid':True}\nfor name in ('selftest','random1','random2','random3'):\n for flags in ((),('--istall','--dstall')):\n  x=run_one(probe/'bench'/f'{name}.elf',sim,str(root),sim_flags=flags)\n  print(x,flush=True)\n  assert x['passed'],x\nsummary['trace_cosim_passed']=True\n(probe/'validation.json').write_text(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nh={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r11s0',\n'title':'Register complete register-file read controls at decode',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 200.25 at median Gowin Fmax 90.355 MHz with\n4,512,213 bracketed CoreMark cycles. A fresh unchanged vendor build\nreproduces that frequency and exposes closely competing ALU, predictor\nmaintenance and operand-delivery paths; one placement also shows MEM/WB\nrd-to-register-file-to-ID/EX operand timing. Earlier r4s1 succeeded by\nregistering bypass provenance, r5s1 preserved real operand flops with\nseparate register-file word banks, and r10s1 added a decoded boundary.\nThat boundary now offers a place to resolve the remaining physical\nregister-file word selects and write-first bypass matches before the\noperand data cycle. Prior micro_opt work on wide EX/MEM capture lost\n4.69 percent, while r9s0 fast-result selection lost 0.59 percent. Keep\nthe successful datapath and optimize its read-selection controls.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Register all physical register-file read-selection controls at the\nexisting decoded-instruction boundary: a 31-bit nonzero-word select\nfor each source plus its one-bit match to the WB instruction that will\nexist during the following operand-read cycle. This is one local\ncontrol-retiming micro_opt. Preserve the pipeline topology, instruction\nlatency, predictor and training policy, arithmetic units, complete\nID/EX operands, single issue and nret=1. Do not combine this with BTB,\nALU, memory-lane or fetch-queue changes.\n\nIn id_stage, decode each incoming raw source field against constant\nregister numbers 1 through 31 and capture those one-hot vectors on the\nsame edge and with the same reset/squash/replace/hold priorities as\ndecoded_q. Capture all zeros for invalid incoming records and x0;\nhold the vectors while the decoded record is held. These are actual\nflops feeding the existing constant-index register-file read leaves,\nnot a combinational decode of decoded_q.rs1_addr or rs2_addr. Keep\nbinary source addresses for RVFI, existing forwarding and x0 handling.\nNo packed architectural bundle changes are needed if the vectors are\nexported as internal module ports.\n\nAlso compute two exact next-cycle write-first matches before that\nedge. The next WB candidate is the current accepted MEM instruction:\nits destination is wb_accepted.rd and its writer qualification is\n\"'!reset && !hold_mem_wb && wb_accepted.w_en. This retains final alignment\ntrap suppression and valid/accepted-memory qualification; it must not\nuse the younger EX destination or an assumed unconditional advance.\nFor a replacing decoded record, compare this candidate to the incoming\nraw source addresses and qualify by incoming validity and nonzero rd.\nFor a held decoded record, compare against decoded_q'\"'s unchanged source\naddresses and validity instead. Reset or accepted recovery clears both\nmatches. Recompute these two small match flops on EVERY nonreset edge,\nincluding decoded holds, because WB can drain or change while the\nconsumer stays decoded. Do not freeze them with the word-select vector.\n\nAfter the edge these match bits must be exactly equivalent to current\nWB write-enable && current WB destination == decoded source && source\n\"'!= x0. An older dmem hold produces an invalid next WB record, hence\nzero matches, while the previously valid WB write still commits to the\nword flops on that edge. Subsequent held reads then see the committed\nregister-file word. A completing blocking producer, load-use bubble,\ntrap-suppressed write, ordinary pipeline bubble or recovery must obey\nthis same real MEM/WB transfer rule. Never retain a match referring to\na departed WB instruction or let a speculative fetch change a held\nconsumer'\"'s match. This preserves the write-first behavior used when a\nproducer leaves the explicit EX/MEM forwarding window.\n\nIn reg_file, retain the 31 resettable word banks, synchronous write\nlogic, x0 override and balanced AND/OR read trees. Feed each tree leaf\nwith its registered word-select bit. For the integrated core, select\nthe existing registered WB data with the supplied registered write-first\nmatch instead of recomputing the live five-bit WB/source equality.\nThe existing EX/accepted-MEM forwarding priority over the register-file\nbase stays unchanged. Do not move data-word capture, add a synchronous\nRAM read, or put a new late destination comparator ahead of the output.\nAn internal parameter may retain the original binary-address interface\nas the default for standalone reg_file unit tests, with the core\nexplicitly enabling predecoded read controls; exercise both modes.\nNeither mode may change actual stored-word writes or x0 behavior.\n\nAn isolated prototype of exactly these three RTL changes passes all\n50 existing pipeline cocotb cases, including deterministic random RVFI\nISS comparison. Gowin placement options 0/1/2 measure 93.390/95.075/\n95.075 MHz, median 95.075 MHz, versus a freshly reproduced champion\nmedian 90.355 MHz. Option-0 resources are 5,730 LUT4 and 2,371 FF versus\n5,971 and 2,307; LUT-RAM/BSRAM/DSP remain 44/4/2. The intended hardware\nchange adds 64 control flops and removes operand-cycle word decoding\nand destination matching. The median-placement worst paths move to\nregistered branch successor/pending-payload formation. This is a\nprototype result, not an accepted tournament measurement.\n\nCoreMark cycles should remain exactly 4,512,213 because every pipeline\ntransfer and prediction is preserved. At that count the prototype\nprojects approximately 210.71 iterations/sec, 5.22 percent above the\nchampion. Acceptance requires median Fmax above 94.51133 MHz at unchanged\ncycles, or that threshold times any measured new/old cycle ratio.\nEstimate five percent higher fitness with medium confidence. The margin\nover the required 4.6 percent is narrow: mapping changes and competing\nbranch, ALU or predictor paths are material risks. Preserve the described\ncircuit and judge actual median vendor Fmax times bracketed throughput.\n\nThe scratch prototype is under cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/\nr11_rf_control; generated artifacts are transient and deleted before\nevaluation, so implement from this description. Retain all existing\nchecks and every external port, full address, RVFI operand/successor,\nretirement order, decoder legality, alignment trap, real/ALTOPS RV32M\nresult and exactly-once memory transaction. Add cycle-by-cycle assertions\nthat registered word selects equal decoded source addresses and that\nregistered write-first matches equal the actual qualified WB comparison.\nExercise all register numbers, x0, simultaneous reads/writes, distinct\nsame-rd producer ages, long decoded/EX/MEM holds, departing WB writers,\nloads and traps, blocking completion, imem bubbles, reset and recovery.\nRequire unchanged formal, stalled instruction cosim, canonical CoreMark\nCRC/UART/marker checks and median vendor timing. No evaluation changes.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':5,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Capture two 31-bit physical RF word-select vectors with the decoded\nrecord. Register exact matches to the next accepted MEM/WB writer,\nusing incoming sources on replacement and held decoded sources on a\nstall; rebase these matches every edge as WB changes. Preserve decoded\nrecord and complete operand-bank transfer/hold/flush behavior.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Consume registered word selects and write-first match bits in an\nexplicitly enabled integrated-core mode, eliminating live read-word\ndecode and WB/source comparison from operand delivery. Retain all\nword flops, reset/write semantics, balanced read trees and x0 priority;\nkeep the original default standalone interface behavior testable.\n'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Wire the registered physical RF read controls from id_stage to\nreg_file. Supply id_stage with the actual next WB destination and\nwriter qualification from wb_accepted and \"'!hold_mem_wb. Enable the\npredecoded RF mode without changing external IO or forwarding ages.\n'\"'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain every architectural assertion and add exact cycle-by-cycle\none-hot/source and write-first/current-WB equivalence assertions,\nincluding held decoded records, changing/departing WB, memory waits,\nload-use, trap suppression, blocking completion, reset and recovery.\n'''},\n{'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Exercise both original binary-address and predecoded-control modes\nfor all word selects, x0, same-cycle write-first forwarding, different\nsource/destination matches, reset and back-to-back writes. Retain\nexisting exact data and x0 assertions; validate supplied selects\nagainst the binary-address reference rather than weakening checks.\n'''}]}\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r11s0.yaml')\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90,default_flow_style=False))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Revised hypothesis written and schema validated:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
