{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_60", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r4s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Fuse forwarding and ALU operand selection with one-hot enables\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion scores 131.70 at median Gowin Fmax 58.072 MHz\n  and 4,409,538 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported worst path starts\n  at cpu/u_ex/reg_q.rd_1_s0, traverses u_fwd, EX and the fast ALU, and\n  ends at data memory after 26 cells and 24.558 ns. The registered\n  multiplier and JALR implementation has removed their competing deep\n  cones, leaving destination-tag comparisons and operand selection on\n  the ordinary single-cycle path. Forwarding currently priority-encodes\n  matches into a two-bit selector, decodes it in a three-way operand mux,\n  then passes that value through a separate PC/immediate mux before ALU\n  arithmetic. The earlier micro_opt divider-sharing and validity-only\n  attempts did not address this remaining selector cone. The dedicated\n  address experiment improved the previous champion but still lost to\n  registered MUL/JALR; this proposal targets the common operand path of\n  the current champion instead of adding an address register.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the binary forwarding selector and cascaded ALU source muxes\n  with mutually exclusive one-hot source enables and flat masked-OR\n  selection. This is one local combinational operand-selection change;\n  retain all pipeline boundaries, instruction latencies and bus holds.\n\n  For each EX source, compute E = ex_mem_w_en && ex_mem_rd != 0 &&\n  ex_mem_rd == source_addr and W = mem_wb_w_en && mem_wb_rd != 0 &&\n  mem_wb_rd == source_addr. Export a three-bit mask whose bit 0 selects\n  the captured ID/EX value, bit 1 selects EX/MEM, and bit 2 selects WB:\n  {(!E && W), E, (!E && !W)}. Exactly one bit is asserted and EX/MEM\n  wins when both writers match. Do not route these enables through a\n  binary encoder or reconstruct them by decoding an encoded selector.\n  Preserve the existing core qualifications for both writer inputs,\n  including WB ctrl.reg_write without WB valid: retained WB data must\n  remain us\n…[3455 of 6527 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4326ba10873bc7bf]…\n execution latencies and interlocks.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Widen internal forwarding wires to three bits and connect the\n      updated interfaces. Keep existing writer qualifications, including\n      retained WB eligibility without valid gating, and every external IO.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Adapt direct EX stimuli to one-hot forwarding masks while retaining\n      every assertion. Check all mask choices and fused ALU source choices,\n      captured architectural operands, slow-operation launch/hold behavior,\n      and JALR target capture under backpressure.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed repeated-destination forwarding priority, independent\n      rs1/rs2 sourcing, x0, immediate/AUIPC override, load/store base and\n      store-data dependencies, and retained WB forwarding during dmem\n      holds. Retain all existing tests and check results, RVFI operands,\n      order, branch behavior and exactly-once memory effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
