{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"agent_message","text":"The explicit flop register-file probe raises option-0 Fmax from 60.645 to 64.335 MHz and removes the block-RAM launch path; all five register-file tests pass. It uses 5,437 LUTs and 2,058 flip-flops. I’m checking the other placement options and pipeline behavior before committing to this angle."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_50", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r5s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r5s0',\n'title':'Use explicit register words and one-hot reads to avoid slow block-RAM operands',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 144.84 at median Gowin Fmax 63.869 MHz and\n4,409,538 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its previous micro_opt attempts\noptimized the divider, validity masking, memory-address separation,\nand forwarding. Registered forwarding provenance now exposes the\nregister file as the common source of the two limiting execute paths.\nA fresh unchanged-RTL option-0 run reproduces 60.645 MHz and the\n21.784 ns worst path exactly. The detailed vendor report starts at a\nregister-file BSRAM output, traverses operand forwarding and the fast\nALU, and reaches data memory; the nearly equal 21.758 ns path reaches\nthe ALU-result register. The memory output's clock-to-Q alone is\n2.260 ns. The compiler has absorbed registered operand reads into\nblock RAM, making its output timing part of the EX path. A local\nregister-file representation change can preserve architectural cycles\nwhile substituting faster flop outputs and retaining explicit ID\noperand capture. An adder-only timing probe gave only 1.4 percent\noption-0 improvement, supporting work on this shared launch point.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace only the register-file storage and combinational read\nimplementation with 31 explicit 32-bit register words, each with a\nconstant-index synchronous write enable, plus one-hot masked-OR reads.\nThe purpose is to avoid inference of a synchronous-read block-RAM\nregister file and preserve the ID/EX operand-register boundary already\npresent in the source. This changes physical storage and read-mux\nmapping, with no logical pipeline stage or instruction-latency change.\n\nUse an elaboration-time generate loop for x1 through x31. Each word\nowns a 32-bit value_q updated at the rising edge: synchronous reset\nclears it; otherwise write only when w_en and w_addr equals that word's\nconstant five-bit index. Wire x0 directly to zero, with no storage or\nwrite enable. For each read port decode its five-bit address into\nmutually exclusive word selects. AND each constant-index word with\nits replicated select, then OR the 32 masked words into the raw read\nresult. Use constant-index references throughout the stored-data read\nnetwork rather than a variable-index array read, so storage stays as\nindividual registers rather than an inferred RAM. Express the OR as\na reduction of the masked words or a balanced tree; do not introduce\npriority encoders. All address and data bits remain architectural.\n\nRetain exactly the current output priorities: an x0 read returns zero;\notherwise a same-cycle enabled write to that read address returns\nw_data; otherwise return the stored raw result. Both read ports remain\ncombinational, writes remain synchronous, and reset still clears every\nwritable register. Keep reg_file's ports and all core wiring unchanged.\nDo not add synthesis keep/dont_touch directives or modify FPGA wrappers.\nPreserve registered one-hot EX forwarding, held-operand refresh,\nretained WB bypass data, load-use interlocks, blocking arithmetic and\nJALR completion, predictor recovery, decoder legality, memory stalls,\nRVFI operands/order/traps, and the nret=1 IO contract.\n\nAn isolated RTL probe of this exact structure measures 64.335 MHz\nwith Gowin placement option 0, versus the reproduced 60.645 MHz base:\n6.08 percent faster. Its worst arrival shortens to 20.838 ns and moves\nfrom register-file RAM to a WB-control-through-branch-recovery path.\nResources are 5,437 LUTs, 2,058 flip-flops, 44 LUT-RAMs, 4 BSRAMs, and\n2 DSPs, versus 3,998/1,084/52/6/2 for the base. Thus the measured change\nremoves the two register-file BSRAMs and changes the limiting cone,\nwith area still below the 10,000-LUT target. All five existing register-\nfile tests pass on the probe. Median placement timing and full-pipeline\nvalidation are still being checked; option-0 alone is not a scored\nfitness result. Ignored probe files are under\ncores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/flop_rf/ and are experimental\nevidence only; the implementation must be rebuilt from this description.\n\nRetain all existing tests and add a register-file scoreboard covering\nall 32 addresses on both ports, concurrent writes/read collisions,\nx0 priority, disabled writes, random data, and reset after writes.\nRequire unchanged pipeline tests, formal, RVFI cosim in zero-wait and\nstalled modes, and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker/OOB gates. Compare\nbracketed cycles to the champion: this semantics-preserving change\nshould keep 4,409,538 cycles. At unchanged cycles, acceptance needs\nmedian Fmax above approximately 66.807 MHz. The estimate is an 8 percent\nfitness gain, with uncertainty from routing the extra flops and the\nnew competing recovery path. Use the evaluator's actual median Gowin\nFmax times bracketed iterations/cycle, without crediting RVFI pruning.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Replace the dynamically indexed storage/read implementation with\n31 constant-index clocked register words, x0 wiring, per-port\none-hot masked-OR read reductions, and the existing write-first\nbypass priority. Preserve synchronous reset, every bit, ports,\ncombinational reads and synchronous writes. Prevent RAM inference\nthrough the RTL structure, without synthesis-preservation attributes.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Retain every existing assertion and add a deterministic scoreboard\nchecking all register addresses on both read ports, concurrent\nwrites and write-first collisions, same-address dual reads, x0\nwrites/reads, disabled writes, random data, and reset after nonzero\ncontents. Keep pipeline, formal and cosim contracts unchanged.\n'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,default_flow_style=False,width=76))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Updated and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
