{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nwt=Path.cwd(); base=wt/'cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/lutram_wb'\nr=build_all(wt,base/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',base/'timing',options=(1,2))\n(base/'results_12.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2))\nr=json.loads((base/'results.json').read_text())+r\nsummary=summarize(r); (base/'summary.json').write_text(json.dumps(summary,indent=2))\nprint(json.dumps(summary),flush=True)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\"placement_failed\": false, \"fmax_mhz\": 67.366, \"seeds\": [65.265, 67.366, 67.366], \"perturbations\": [[0, 65.265, 14], [1, 67.366, 13], [2, 67.366, 13]], \"lut4\": 4237, \"ff\": 1064, \"lutram\": 76, \"bsram\": 4, \"dsp\": 2, \"logic_levels\": 14, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/fwd_rs1_sel_0_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -10.322, \"arrival_ns\": 20.617, \"logic_cells\": 17, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"agent_message","text":"The combined operand-delivery probe reaches 65.265 MHz on option 0, with 4,237 LUTs and 1,064 flip-flops. It passes the register-file and pipeline suites, eight RVFI cosim runs, and stalled CoreMark with canonical CRCs and the champion’s exact 4,409,538 measured cycles. The median timing run is still pending."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_69", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,yaml,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r5s0.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-001-r5s0',\n'title':'Preserve fast operand delivery with distributed register RAM and registered writeback selection',\n'category':'micro_opt',\n'motivation':'''The champion scores 144.84 at median Gowin Fmax 63.869 MHz with\n4,409,538 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior micro_opt attempts addressed\ndivision, bubble masking, memory-address separation, and forwarding;\nnone controlled register-file memory inference and writeback-data\nplacement together. A fresh unchanged option-0 run reproduces\n60.645 MHz and the 21.784 ns worst path. Detailed vendor timing shows\nregister-file block RAM launching both limiting execute paths, with\n2.260 ns clock-to-Q. Replacing it with flops alone merely exposes the\nWB load/ALU mux and does not improve median Fmax. Distributed RAM alone\nalso exposes that mux. These competing paths motivate one focused\noperand-delivery optimization: preserve the existing ID operand-capture\nboundary with asynchronous distributed register RAM, and select the\nfinal WB value before its register rather than on the EX bypass path.\n''',\n'hypothesis':'''Retain the existing pipeline and instruction timing, but restructure\nregister-file and writeback operand delivery so EX sources come from\nexplicitly registered values without a block-RAM output or late\nload-versus-ALU selection. Both parts address the same operand-delivery\ncone; do not change arithmetic, prediction, fetch buffering, or issue\npolicy.\n\nIn reg_file, infer the 32-by-32 array as asynchronous distributed RAM\nusing Gowin's supported syn_ramstyle=\\\"distributed_ram\\\" attribute. Keep\nthe single synchronous write and both combinational reads. Remove the\nbulk reset of stored RAM data, which prevents the desired inference,\nand replace it with a resettable 32-bit written_q vector. Reset clears\nall written bits and suppresses the RAM write; a normal enabled write\nto x1..x31 sets its written bit and stores all 32 data bits. A read of\nan unwritten register returns zero, making architectural reset behavior\nidentical even though stale physical RAM contents remain. Preserve the\nexact existing priorities: x0 always reads zero; otherwise a same-cycle\nenabled matching write returns w_data; otherwise a written register\nreturns its RAM word, and an unwritten register returns zero. Writes\nto x0 are ignored. A later reset must hide every prior stored word\nuntil it is written again. Keep ports and full address/data widths.\nThe asynchronous read feeds the existing ID/EX operand capture; do\nnot add a read cycle or change the registered forwarding provenance.\n\nAdd a wb_data field to mem_wb_t. At ordinary MEM/WB capture register\nin.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result into this field, after\nthe existing load alignment and sign/zero extension. Reset clears it;\nduring hold_wb retain it together with the other data fields while\nclearing retirement valid exactly as today. wb_stage's w_data becomes\na direct wire from this registered field. Preserve its w_en and\nw_addr, the effective next-WB forwarding metadata, and the retained\nWB-data eligibility after valid clears. Existing alu_result/read_data\nfields may remain for compatibility; remove their combinational\nwriteback selection from the timed operand path. This moves selection\nacross an existing register boundary, with no new stage or extra cycle.\n\nKeep decoder legality, real and ALTOPS arithmetic, predictor training\nand recovery, held-operand refresh, load-use bubbles, MUL/DIV/JALR\ncapture/completion, dmem handshakes, traps, RVFI order and operands,\nand nret=1 top-level IO unchanged. Do not alter evaluation sources or\nforce placement. Use the RAM inference attribute only for the desired\nstorage primitive, without keep/dont_touch directives.\n\nAn isolated probe of this combined structure measures 65.265 MHz on\nGowin placement option 0 versus the reproduced 60.645 MHz base,\na 7.62 percent gain. Worst arrival shortens from 21.784 to 20.617 ns;\nthe critical launch moves off register-file RAM and WB selection to\nregistered forwarding controls. Resources are 4,237 LUTs, 1,064 FF,\n76 LUT-RAMs, 4 BSRAMs, and 2 DSPs, versus 3,998/1,084/52/6/2 for the\nbase. Both register-file BSRAMs disappear, without the large flop\nincrease of the unsuccessful explicit-register implementation.\nMedian placement timing is still pending; option 0 is not the score.\n\nThe probe passes all five register-file tests and all 38 pipeline\ntests. Full RVFI comparisons pass selftest and three random programs,\neach with zero waits and with both random bus-stall streams, eight\nruns total. Stalled canonical CoreMark reports all five expected CRCs,\nthe validation UART literal, both timing markers, no OOB, and exactly\n4,409,538 bracketed cycles. Probe RTL and reports reside under\ncores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/lutram_wb/; they are ignored\nexperimental evidence, not implementation deliverables. Reimplement\nfrom this description and require all unchanged evaluation gates,\nincluding formal, which has not been run on the probe. At unchanged\ncycles acceptance needs median Fmax above approximately 66.807 MHz.\nRouting of the written-bit mux and the next recovery/control path\nremain uncertainties; judge actual median vendor Fmax times measured\niterations/cycle, not individual placement results or RVFI pruning.\n''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'medium'},\n'changes':[\n {'file':'rtl/reg_file.sv','description':'''Infer asynchronous distributed RAM with syn_ramstyle and replace\nbulk data reset with resettable per-register written bits. Preserve\nall-zero architectural reset, reset-time write suppression, x0,\nsynchronous writes, dual combinational reads, full bit widths, and\nthe existing same-cycle write-first output priority. Ensure stale\nRAM contents cannot become visible after any reset.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Add a 32-bit wb_data field to mem_wb_t for the final selected\narchitectural writeback value. Preserve every other payload field,\nretirement width, and external interface.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/mem_stage.sv','description':'''Select load_data versus in.alu_result before MEM/WB and capture\nthe result in wb_data. Clear on reset and retain across hold_wb;\nkeep valid-clearing, load formatting, traps, memory side effects,\nRVFI metadata and next-WB forwarding eligibility unchanged.\n'''},\n {'file':'rtl/wb_stage.sv','description':'''Drive w_data directly from registered wb_data, removing the late\nmem_to_reg mux from WB-to-EX and WB-to-ID operand delivery. Preserve\nw_en validity qualification, destination address, and held data.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_reg_file.py','description':'''Retain all assertions and add deterministic scoreboard coverage\nof all addresses on both ports, same-cycle collisions, x0,\ndisabled writes, unwritten-zero reads, and reset after nonzero\ncontents followed by partial rewrites. Check that stale RAM data\nstays hidden and write-first priority matches the prior behavior.\n'''},\n {'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain existing assertions and add directed alternating LOAD/ALU\nWB dependencies, byte/halfword sign extension, retained WB data\nthrough long memory holds, reset after register writes, and\nblocking-operation completion dependencies. Verify exact RVFI\noperands/results, order, and exactly-once effects with both bus\nstall streams. Require unchanged formal/cosim/CoreMark gates.\n'''}]\n}\nclass LiteralDumper(yaml.SafeDumper): pass\ndef rep(dumper,s): return dumper.represent_scalar('tag:yaml.org,2002:str',s,style='|' if '\\\\n' in s else None)\nLiteralDumper.add_representer(str,rep)\np.write_text(yaml.dump(d,Dumper=LiteralDumper,sort_keys=False,width=88))\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised and validated',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
