{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-001-r6s0.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-001-r6s0\ntitle: \"Register final writeback data before the forwarding network\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion reaches 70.604 MHz and 160.12 CoreMark iterations/sec.\n  Its reported worst path launches from MEM/WB's mem_to_reg control, crosses\n  the writeback mux and EX forwarding, and ends at a predictor counter.\n  Selecting between two registered 32-bit results on every WB fanout adds\n  logic before branch comparison, ALU inputs, and register-file bypass.\n  The r5s0 micro_opt experiment moved this selection before MEM/WB together\n  with a distributed-RAM register file and reached only 67.366 MHz. The\n  winning r5s1 flop-bank register file avoids the RAM operand delay but\n  retains the late WB mux. Isolating early final-data selection on that\n  winning storage topology targets the measured timing cone without\n  changing instruction throughput or adding another register-file gamble.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move only the load-versus-ALU writeback selection across the existing\n  MEM/WB register boundary. Add a 32-bit wb_data field to mem_wb_t and\n  register in.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result in mem_stage,\n  after the existing byte extraction and sign/zero extension. Drive\n  wb_stage.w_data directly from this registered value. All consumers of\n  wb_w_data, including EX forwarding, ID write-first bypass, physical\n  register writes and RVFI rd_wdata, then see the same final data without\n  a control-dependent mux after MEM/WB. Keep the current flop-bank register\n  file, registered one-hot forwarding provenance and pipeline depth.\n\n  Reset clears wb_data. A dmem hold preserves it with the other retained\n  MEM/WB data fields while clearing valid exactly as today. Preserve the\n  distinction between forwarding eligibility of held WB data and valid\n  retirement/regfile writes. Capture final data on every ordinary MEM/WB\n  advance, including ALU, load, JAL/JALR and completed M-extension results;\n  do not gate data capture on valid or introduce a new acceptance rule.\n  Existing alu_\n…[1087 of 4159 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:bae283407a07d6e8]…\nretaining existing\n      architectural and RVFI metadata and the nret=1 interface.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Register the selected, fully extended load data or ALU result into\n      wb_data at ordinary MEM/WB capture. Clear it on reset and retain it\n      during hold_wb; preserve current valid clearing, traps, forwarding\n      metadata, memory masks and exactly-once bus behavior.\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the combinational mem_to_reg mux with a direct assignment\n      from registered wb_data. Preserve destination and valid-qualified\n      write enable, so every WB consumer receives the same final result.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain existing assertions and cover alternating ALU/load WB sources,\n      signed and unsigned subword loads, WB-to-EX and write-first bypass,\n      competing writers, prolonged dmem holds, blocking completions and\n      reset. Check exact RVFI operands/results/order and single memory effects.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
