{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_17", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261002-001-r6s0','hyp-20261002-002-r8s1']:\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-001-r6s0 Register complete ALU operand choices ahead of execute\nMake each EX ALU operand a single masked-OR selection from registered\ndata, with its complete source choice registered at the ID/EX edge.\nThis is one operand-delivery timing optimization: remove late WB data\nselection and serial forwarding-then-PC/immediate selection. Preserve\npipeline depth, arithmetic and instruction acceptance timing.\n\nRegister final WB data before the existing MEM/WB boundary. Add wb_data\nto mem_wb_t; on ordinary MEM capture assign\nin.ctrl.mem_to_reg ? load_data : in.alu_result, using existing aligned\nand sign/zero-extended load_data. WB w_data becomes a direct wire from\nwb_data. Reset clears it; hold_wb retains it while valid clears exactly\nas today. Preserve destination/write metadata and held-WB forwarding\neligibility. Existing alu_result/read_data fields can remain, but must\nnot recreate the late WB mux for any timed consumer.\n\nAlongside existing three-bit raw-register forwarding selections,\nregister independent four-bit one-hot ALU source selections in core.\nBits 0,1,2 choose ID/EX base, next EX/MEM producer and next MEM/WB\nproducer; bit 3 chooses PC for A or immediate for B. Expose just\nnext_alu_a_pc and next_alu_b_imm from id_stage's exact ID/EX next-control\nchoice: reset or flush clears them; otherwise a hold uses current\nregistered is_auipc/alu_src, and an advance uses in.valid-qualified\ndecoded controls. Do not route the entire ID/EX next-state datapath.\n\nOn reset or flush initialize both ALU selectors to 4'b0001. Otherwise\nregister A as {next_alu_a_pc, next_fwd_rs1 & {3{!next_alu_a_pc}}} and B\nas {next_alu_b_imm, next_fwd_rs2 & {3{!next_alu_b_imm}}}. Refresh every\nedge, including holds, exactly as raw forwarding provenance does now.\nMatch actual producers entering or remaining in EX/MEM and MEM/WB;\ndo not substitute current-cycle destination tags.\n\nForm alu_a directly as the OR of four masked values: in.rs1_val,\nfwd_ex_mem, fwd_mem_wb and in.pc. Form alu_b similarly from in.rs2_val,\nfwd_ex_mem, fwd_mem_wb \n…[2980 of 6052 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:512fdd30405524a8]…\npaired with the original EX/MEM retirement\nmetadata. Preserve exactly-once memory effects, misalignment traps\nand suppressed writes, load-use bubbling, held-WB forwarding and\naccepted-load forwarding. Nonmemory MEM records still accept and\nresolve independently of unrelated low dmemReady. No added request\ncycle, store buffer, speculative data access or idle-address fallback\nto the scalar result is permitted.\n\nPreserve all core ports and nret=1, default-illegal decoding, complete\nRVFI operands and successor PCs, contiguous retirement order and\nreal/ALTOPS RV32M arithmetic. Retain every existing assertion and run\nunit, unchanged formal and instruction cosim gates plus canonical\nstalled CoreMark CRC/UART/marker validation. Measure median Gowin\nFmax times actual bracketed iterations/cycle. At unchanged cycles,\nacceptance requires more than 87.795 MHz; 94 MHz would yield about\n12 percent fitness improvement. Competing scalar bypass, decode and\npredictor paths may cap the gain; this is an estimate pending timing.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
