{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('category') == 'structural':\n  print(d['id'],d['title'])\n  print('Fitness', d.get('fitness'), 'Fmax', d.get('fmax_mhz'),'cycles',d.get('cycles'),'critical',d.get('critical_path'))\n  h=d.get('hypothesis',{})\n  if isinstance(h,dict): h=h.get('hypothesis', '')\n  print(h[:3000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-002-r1s1 Isolate division in a bounded multicycle execution unit\nFitness 98.49 Fmax 44.239 cycles 4491817 critical {'from': 'cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/id_ex_w.rs2_val_0_s100', 'slack_ns': -19.082, 'arrival_ns': 29.376, 'logic_cells': 52, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']}\nReplace only DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU with one shared, bounded multicycle\nradix-2 restoring divider, leaving RV32I operations and multiplication on\ntheir existing fast path. Register the fully forwarded operands and the\noperation once when a valid divide instruction can start. Use registered\nmagnitude preparation, a 33-bit trial subtraction and one quotient-bit\nupdate per iteration for exactly 32 iterations, and registered sign\ncorrection. Use the trial subtraction's borrow to choose the restored\nremainder instead of cascading a separate comparison and subtraction.\nBoth quotient and\nremainder come from the same unsigned division state; select and sign them\naccording to the original instruction. Eliminate all synthesizable / and\n% operators from the core ALU. Division by zero and signed overflow must\nreturn the exact RV32M results, with a bounded completion handshake.\n\nKeep the divide instruction in EX while it runs. Allow older EX/MEM and\nMEM/WB instructions to drain, injecting fully zeroed bubbles into EX/MEM\nwhen it is free, with valid, mem_read, mem_write, and reg_write all zero.\nThis prevents holding and replaying an older memory transaction.\nHold fetch and ID/EX until the divide result transfers exactly once into\nEX/MEM. Capture forwarded operands at launch so retiring WB data cannot\ndisappear during the wait; retain the original instruction, PC, register\naddresses, and captured operand values for RVFI. A completed result must\nremain pending across dmem backpressure, with no relaunch or duplicate\nretirement. Gate redirects and memory side effects by valid instruction\ntransfer, and preserve the existing load-use and WB forwarding rules.\n\nKeep the core's nr\n…[32432 of 35504 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9a434dfc261ef3e1]…\nning acceptance\nboundary; table commit occurs one edge later as described below.\nRedirect dominates bus stalls and all queue holds, sets the full fetch\nPC to the resolved successor and invalidates every queued younger\ninstruction on that edge. Suppress enqueue and dequeue on recovery.\nAlso retain the existing clearing of decoded/EX positions and pending\nblocking units, while the resolving instruction still retires once.\nEach queued record retains its original prediction snapshot even if\ntraining changes the BTB before dequeue. Prediction selects the next\nfetch PC when that record is enqueued; later mismatch recovery uses\nthat same snapshot. Do not add a speculative outcome bypass or a\nsecond predictor policy. Reset clears queue validity and restores\nRESET_PC with the current predictor reset discipline.\n\nAt training acceptance, register the event's exact index/full tag,\neligibility, jump/outcome and aligned full target, together with the\nselected table row's valid bit, full tag and two-bit direction state.\nTa\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
