{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"Moving partial sums ahead of EX also lost timing: it lengthened the register-file-to-operand-capture path. I’m now checking an alternating EX/MEM register-bank structure to preserve the result register boundary before FPGA memory. It keeps the current instruction latency and dependency rules."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_39", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r12s1',\n'title':'Alternate physical EX/MEM banks to preserve the execution register boundary',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 223.34 iterations/sec at median 95.961 MHz and 4,296,564 bracketed cycles. Its worst reported path starts at ID/EX operand flops and ends at the FPGA data-memory bank through the ALU and EX capture logic. Prior r5s1 established that the vendor can absorb CPU registers into memory ports, and explicit storage topology can restore a useful physical boundary. A separate memory lane failed in r8s1 and a fresh current-parent probe also regressed to 90.471 MHz at unchanged cycles, exposing RF/operand delivery. Moving half-sums before the existing operand flops regressed to 63.290 MHz by lengthening RF-to-capture. Preserve the current datapath and timing boundaries instead, and change the physical organization of the EX/MEM register itself.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Replace the single physical EX/MEM payload bank with two separately declared resettable flop banks and a registered active-bank selector. On every non-stalled EX/MEM update, write the inactive bank with the EXACT existing reg_d value, then toggle the selector on that same edge. The selected bank is the logical EX/MEM instruction; all MEM, recovery/training, held-producer and verification consumers see that selected record. During a genuine older data-memory hold, preserve both banks and the selector. Reset zeroes both banks and selects bank zero. There is no extra pipeline stage, transaction buffering, dual issue, speculative side effect or additional accepted instruction.\n\nThis is one physical pipeline-register topology change. The two distinct storage banks and the real output selection aim to prevent address/result registers from being folded directly into the FPGA memory's input register and to isolate arithmetic capture from memory-port timing. Use separate concrete bank variables, not a variable-index inferred RAM or a keep/dont_touch attribute. The output word must actually select between the two stored records; never add an ALU-result shortcut to the memory port. Retain the existing fast/completed/held reg_d construction and all concrete forwarding producers, so arithmetic and operand capture are unchanged.\n\nWriting the inactive bank precedes selecting it logically on the same clock edge: the active record after any update must equal the old implementation's next EX/MEM record bit for bit. Advance the selector on every non-stalled update, including an invalid bubble or accepted recovery squash, because the selected bank must show that same fully zeroed bubble. Do not expose a retained bank without overwriting it. A held transaction keeps its address, store data, masks and enables stable until acceptance. Accepted older recovery still sends the resolving record to MEM/WB while replacing younger EX work with the exact squash bubble; pending arithmetic cancellation and capture/consume priorities remain unchanged. Both banks must reset even though only one is initially selected.\n\nKeep recovery_q's logical update semantics and its association with the selected instruction. Train and redirect only on existing accepted-MEM qualification, with exactly one event per instruction. The active-bank selector must not change acceptance or instruction ordering. Every actual fast/completed next producer and early destination match must describe the same instruction as reg_d. Preserve the decoded metadata/complete operand banks, source provenance, unavailable-load hazard, WB write-first behavior, M and JALR holds, frontend queue and predictor learning. Preserve all nret=1 ports, full address and PC widths, default-illegal and SYSTEM decoding, alignment traps, real/ALTOPS RV32M results, all RVFI operands/successors/memory fields, contiguous order and exactly-once stores.\n\nCheck vendor timing and resource use: retaining two banks costs additional flops and an output mux, and RF/forwarding, recovery, or memory-response paths may become critical. Accept only a measured gain over the unchanged vendor median Fmax times marker-bracketed CoreMark throughput. With unchanged cycles, median Fmax must exceed 100.375 MHz for the 4.6 percent acceptance margin. The estimate is 8 percent higher fitness with low confidence pending the scratch timing probe and full implementation gates.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Declare two physical EX/MEM flop banks and an active-bank selector. Write inactive storage with existing reg_d and swap selection on every non-stalled update; retain both banks and selection on older memory holds. Reset both banks. Keep logical reg_q as the exact selected payload and preserve recovery, pending operations, effective next-state summaries and concrete producers. Expose no live result bypass to MEM.'''} ,\n{'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Retain direct EX assertions and add cycle-by-cycle equality between the selected bank and the original reg_d/update reference. Cover both selector phases, fast arithmetic, loads/stores, bubbles, completion, stalls, reset and dominant recovery, including stale data retained only in inactive storage.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Retain exact architectural assertions and verify alternating banks do not alter retirement cycles, dependencies, forwarding ages, load-use bubbles or MEM acceptance. Cover consecutive requests, long data holds, blocking launches/completion, prediction recovery, reset in both bank phases and bubble sequences. Check stable held requests, exactly-once stores/training, exact RVFI and strict order. Run unchanged unit/formal/ISS cosim and canonical stalled CoreMark CRC/UART/marker gates, then median Gowin timing.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=78))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Updated hypothesis is schema-valid:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
