{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"agent_message","text":"Alternating EX/MEM banks prevents Gowin from inferring the benchmark’s block RAM, so that approach cannot fit the FPGA. I’m testing one final stage repartition: capture register-file reads with decoded metadata, compute short arithmetic chunks during operand read, then finish carry selection in EX. This keeps the existing pipeline depth while avoiding a register-file read tree followed by a long adder."}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-002-r12s1.yaml')\nd={\n'id':'hyp-20261002-002-r12s1',\n'title':'Balance registered execution across decode, operand read, and EX',\n'category':'structural',\n'motivation':'''The champion reaches 223.34 iterations/sec at median 95.961 MHz and 4,296,564 bracketed CoreMark cycles. Its reported critical path begins at complete ID/EX operands and reaches FPGA data memory through the shared ALU and EX capture logic. Previous structural work successfully isolated slow M arithmetic, complete operands, decoded metadata, and frontend ingress. The existing decoded stage now creates an unused opportunity to pre-read the register file before operand resolution, allowing short arithmetic work to move across the operand/EX boundary without adding a stage. A current-parent memory-lane probe regressed to 90.471 MHz; simply precomputing 16-bit half-sums after the existing RF read regressed to 63.290 MHz. Thus repartition the existing stages together: first register RF snapshots, then compute short resolved-operand chunks, then finish execution.''',\n'hypothesis':'''Repartition the work of the existing decode, operand-read, and EX stages, retaining their number and every instruction-transfer boundary. The new topology is registered fetch head -> decode plus RF snapshots -> forwarding plus eight-bit arithmetic partials -> EX carry selection and result capture -> MEM -> WB. This is one structural pipeline-balancing change; do not add a cache, predictor policy, general issue stage, or instruction bubble.\n\nDuring decode capture, read the existing flop-bank register file using the registered fetch head's raw rs1/rs2 fields. Register both read words alongside the matching decoded metadata. Keep the explicit resettable RF word flops and balanced trees; avoid inferred RAM that absorbs downstream operand registers. Raw head fields drive only this earlier RF lookup, never the late EX operand mux. The RF's existing current-WB write-first behavior applies to these reads. Capture or hold the snapshots with the same decoded record, and clear them on reset/accepted recovery.\n\nAt operand resolution, use the captured RF snapshots as base values instead of traversing the RF word-selection tree again. A newer current MEM/WB writer may have appeared after the snapshot: retain an explicit qualified current-WB-over-snapshot bypass, matching the held decoded source address and excluding x0. During every decoded hold, refresh a matching snapshot from an actual qualified WB write so a departed producer cannot be lost. Keep the existing concrete EX fast/link/completed and accepted-MEM bypasses, registered provenance, youngest-writer priority, unavailable-load interlock and rebasing rules. Current-WB repair is older than EX and accepted MEM. Never freeze a stale RF snapshot through a hold or replace a source merely because a younger unavailable load matches.\n\nFrom the same fully resolved complete ALU operands that currently enter ID/EX, compute registered eight-bit carry-select partials. For ADD use B unchanged and initial carry zero. For SUB, SLT and SLTU use B complemented and initial carry one. Register a nine-bit low-byte sum including its carry, plus two nine-bit sums for each higher byte, evaluated independently for assumed carry zero and one. All additions are explicitly widened to nine bits. Capture these seven partial words on exactly the ID/EX operand edge, hold them with the instruction, and reset/flush them with its existing priorities. Preserve full raw rs1/rs2 and complete ALU operands for RVFI, logic/shifts, branches and the M units.\n\nEX reconstructs the 32-bit result by selecting each higher byte and outgoing carry from its registered carry-zero/carry-one candidates. Only a short byte-carry selection chain remains after the operand register; no fresh 32-bit ripple addition is allowed on the integrated ADD/SUB/address/JALR path. ADD/SUB use the reconstructed modulo-32 result. SLTU uses the inverse of the final subtraction carry. SLT uses operand-sign comparison when signs differ and reconstructed subtraction sign otherwise. Use the reconstructed ADD for ordinary load/store effective addresses, AUIPC and JALR target arithmetic. Keep JALR's existing pending capture/bit-zero clearing, alignment checks, and completion latency. Leave direct PC+immediate target preparation and PC+4 links on their existing paths. Logic/shifts use the unchanged complete operand flops. Keep standalone ALU exact-bit behavior and clocked real/ALTOPS M arithmetic; prune the unused full-width integrated arithmetic path rather than feeding it back through a fallback mux.\n\nKeep the existing fast/link/completed producer separation, EX/MEM emission priorities, registered MEM recovery/training, data-memory hold and accepted-load forwarding cycles. Both forwarded arithmetic results and the captured EX/MEM arithmetic result must use the same reconstructed value. No consumer may see a partial sum. The desired recurrence is registered byte candidates -> short result selection -> forwarding -> eight-bit partial addition -> candidate registers, while the RF read tree ends a stage earlier. The principal risk is moving too much work into operand read or exposing decode, shifts, memory-return, or bus-control timing. Judge actual vendor timing; register count alone is irrelevant.\n\nPreserve nret=1 and every external port, full addresses/PCs, decoder default-illegal and SYSTEM policy, alignment traps, original instruction operands and successor PCs, real and ALTOPS RV32M behavior, contiguous RVFI order and exactly-once memory side effects. Keep all existing correctness assertions; adapt packed ID/EX layouts only to the additional partial fields. Require unchanged formal, stalled instruction cosim and canonical CoreMark CRC/UART/marker validation. At unchanged 4,296,564 bracketed cycles, acceptance requires median Gowin Fmax above 100.375 MHz. Estimate an eight percent fitness gain with low confidence pending the scratch probe and implementation gates.''',\n'expected_impact':{'fitness_delta_pct':8,'confidence':'low'},\n'changes':[\n{'file':'rtl/core_pkg.sv','description':'''Extend id_ex_t with explicitly widened low-byte and carry-zero/carry-one higher-byte arithmetic partials. Keep complete operands and all existing instruction/RVFI fields; ensure any width-dependent test packing accounts for the added 63 bits.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/id_stage.sv','description':'''Capture RF snapshots alongside decoded records, refresh them from qualified WB writes during holds, and expose current-WB-repaired base words for existing forwarding. Generate and register seven nine-bit add/sub candidates from resolved complete operands at the current ID/EX edge. Retain exact reset, squash, hold, load-use and provenance priorities.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/core.sv','description':'''Drive the existing RF read ports from the fixed registered fetch head for earlier read capture, connect snapshots/current-WB repair through id_stage to forward_unit's base inputs, and retain all existing producer/hazard wiring and external IO.'''},\n{'file':'rtl/ex_stage.sv','description':'''Reconstruct ADD/SUB and signed/unsigned compare results using registered byte candidates and a short carry-selection chain. Use the identical reconstructed arithmetic for fast forwarding, EX/MEM, memory addresses, AUIPC and JALR pending target capture. Leave logic/shifts, links, blocking M units, emission, recovery and memory-acceptance priorities intact. Avoid a full-width arithmetic bypass.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_ex_stage.py','description':'''Retain direct EX checks and populate/check the new partial fields from independent exact arithmetic references. Cover byte carry/borrow propagation, wraparound, signed compare boundaries, unsigned borrow, ADD/SUB/SLT/SLTU, ADD-based addresses/AUIPC/JALR, partial association through holds, squash, reset and blocking completion.'''},\n{'file':'test/test_pipeline.py','description':'''Adapt packed layout to the added fields while retaining assertions. Exercise early RF snapshots with current-WB repair, multiple same-rd producer ages, disappearing WB during long decoded holds, x0, dependent arithmetic chains, every byte carry, negative memory offsets, load-use and dmem waits, blocking operations and recovery. Check exact RVFI operands/results/memory fields/order, stable requests and exactly-once stores. Require existing formal/ISS/CoreMark and median Gowin gates.'''}]\n}\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(d,sort_keys=False,width=78))\njsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint('Balanced-execution hypothesis is schema valid.')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
