{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r2s0'):\n  print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Represent fetch/decode bubbles solely through the existing valid bit,\nremoving late redirect and imem-stall signals from instruction payload\nmasking and from the synchronous clear of ID/EX data. This is one local\ncontrol-cone optimization, with the same pipeline boundaries, arithmetic\nunits, branch penalty, memory interface, and nret=1 retirement contract.\n\nIn if_stage, always expose raw imem_data as out.instr. Retain the current\nout.valid qualification and PC update priorities: reset first, accepted\nEX redirect next, then ordinary advance when not stalled. Decode,\nimmediate generation, and register-file read addresses must depend on\nthe raw fetched instruction rather than a redirect-selected NOP.\n\nIn id_stage, preserve the full reset initialization, but a nonreset flush\nmust clear only reg_q.valid. Give the non-valid payload fields a separate\nupdate condition based only on !stall; they may capture raw data on a\nflush when not stalled, or retain old data when stalled. Neither flush\nnor input validity may select zero or disable capture for those fields.\nUpdate valid with reset/flush priority, otherwise capture in.valid when\n!stall, otherwise hold. This removes the late flush signal from the\nwide operand, immediate, address, instruction, and control register muxes.\n\nSince IF no longer supplies NOP register addresses on invalid fetches,\nqualify the existing load-use comparison with imem_ready. Continue to\nqualify the EX load through id_ex_w.valid at the core connection. Use\nraw instruction rs fields without redirect-dependent masking or gating\nby IF validity, which itself includes redirect. A valid EX load and a\nvalid EX redirect are mutually exclusive in this decoder; redirect\nalready overrides the PC hold and flushes ID/EX. Preserve dmem-stall and\nexecute-busy hold priority over a load-use bubble, and preserve the\nexisting MEM/WB data retention needed for forwarding during dmem holds.\n\nAudit all consumers of possibly stale invalid ID/EX fields. Divider\nrequest/consume, execute_busy, redirects, load-use detection, memory\nside effects, and retirement must continue to require instruction\nvalidity. EX must still emit its existing fully zeroed EX/MEM bubble for\ninvalid input, so invalid decoded mem_read, mem_write, or reg_write bits\nnever become live downstream. Keep decoder default-illegal behavior,\nmisalignment traps, RV32M and ALTOPS arithmetic, and RVFI order unchanged.\n\nThe prediction is unchanged bracketed CoreMark cycles with roughly a\n20 percent fitness gain from a shorter and less heavily loaded recovery\npath. This is an estimate, not a measured result. Require existing unit,\nformal, and stalled CoreMark cosim gates, exact canonical CRCs, and the\nunchanged Gowin median timing evaluation. Check whether the reported\nredirect-to-decode path disappears; a new arithmetic or memory critical\npath may limit the gain. Acceptance still requires more than 4.6 percent\nfitness improvement over 98.49, rather than an incidental placement gain.\n\nAn exhaustive software control model checked 1,310,720 legal combinations\nof load destination and fetched source addresses, redirect/divide state,\nboth ready signals, and pending memory operations. The proposed\nimem_ready-qualified raw-address load-use condition matched the current\nNOP-masked condition throughout, using the decoder's mutual exclusion\nof an EX load with an EX redirect or division. This supports the local\ninterlock transformation; it does not prove RTL correctness or timing.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
