0261002-002-r1s1 (improvement, +712.62%): A shared registered 32-step DIV/REM divider removes the deep combinational EX cone, allowing Fmax gains to outweigh divide stalls when CoreMark divisions occur mainly during initialization outside the timed loop.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r1s2 (regression, +9.32%): A 32-entry tagged BTB cut CoreMark cycles by 6.3%, but forwarding through the combinational M-extension ALU still limi\n…[2983 of 6055 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:efa84e211b21a928]…\nan blunt isolated mux optimizations.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r6s2 (regression, +4.65%): ID-prequalified mismatch bits and registered BTB training cut branch-to-counter logic without delaying recovery, but their throughput gain was edged by WB/ALU operand-selection retiming (+4.74%), so predictor maintenance and operand delivery remain competing timing targets.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r7s1 (improvement, +11.64%): Registering complete ID/EX operands and EX/MEM recovery together isolates EX from incoming bypass selection and outgoing frontend feedback, while prediction limits the extra recovery bubble to misses so Fmax gains outweigh CoreMark's cycle penalty.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r7s2 (regression, -13.12%): Replacing indexed BTB muxes with parallel full-tag flop banks and local hysteresis exposed a fetch-PC-to-ID prediction-mismatch critical path, lowering median Fmax to 64.252 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles; balanced lookup trees must be timed together with decode's target qualification.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
