{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261002-003-r12s2','hyp-20261002-003-r11s2']:\n  print(d['id'])\n  print(d['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis') if isinstance(d['hypothesis'],dict) else d['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r11s2\nRetiming only BTB maintenance should remove the measured counter-update\nbottleneck without adding instruction pipeline stages or fetch queues.\nKeep the 32-entry fully tagged target buffer, indexed fetch tag/target\nreads, full PC/target widths, current prediction snapshots and existing\naccepted-MEM recovery latency. Probe the training tag into one registered\nevent stage inside if_stage, then apply its update independently. Implement\ndirection state as separate constant-index two-flop banks with local\ntransition equations. Keep tag/target arrays\nindexed; do not reproduce the rejected parallel full-tag fetch tree.\n\nEach accepted MEM training event captures valid, PC index/full tag,\naligned eligibility, jump classification, actual outcome, aligned\nfull target and an ordered tag-hit result. The previous event applies\non the next clock while a new event is captured, sustaining one event\nper clock. Events drain despite\nfrontend holds and bus stalls; a redirect preserves its resolving\ninstruction's event. Reset clears event validity and all BTB valid bits.\nDo not delay redirect, retirement, execution, or architectural effects.\nCapture event payload only when train_valid is asserted; event_valid_q\nitself captures train_valid every nonreset clock so bubbles drain it.\nUse a five-bit event_index_q, 25-bit event_tag_q, 30-bit event_target_q,\nand event_valid_q/event_eligible_q/event_jump_q/event_taken_q/event_hit_q.\nOnly event_valid_q needs reset; all table-update gates also reject reset.\n\nEncode each direction counter as direction D and strength S:\nstrongly-not-taken=(0,1), weakly-not-taken=(0,0), weakly-taken=(1,0),\nstrongly-taken=(1,1). A matching conditional event with outcome T uses\nD_next = S ? D : T and S_next = (D == T), both from OLD local state.\nA miss allocates D=T,S=0; a jump writes D=1,S=1. These equations exactly\nimplement the existing two-bit saturation without a selected counter\nread, adder or saturation comparator on the write path. Decode the\nregistered event index against each constant\n…[8337 of 11409 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:91e95a814c1eef14]…\n iterations/sec.\n\nExploratory evidence: a fresh unmodified champion run reproduces exactly\n4,296,564 timed cycles. Straight expansion to an unbanked 128-row array\nuses 4,228,278 cycles but measures only 95.003 MHz median, insufficient for\nacceptance. Taken-only allocation with the original 32-entry organization\nuses 4,221,644 cycles. Both simulations complete with all canonical CRCs,\nsuccess UART, valid markers and no OOB. Correct-path replay estimates\n43,641 control-transfer misses for the proposed hashed 128-entry taken-only\npolicy versus the measured 73,619 in the champion. Replay ignores learning\nlatency and wrong-path fetch and is not a measured cycle or fitness gain.\nExplicit 32-row banking is essential because the unbanked capacity probe\ninferred extra BSRAM and exposed tag-read-to-valid-update timing paths.\nReproduce the proposed combined policy and measure its actual cycle count\nand median vendor timing; exploratory artifacts are not implementation\ninputs and do not establish tournament acceptance.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
