{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'git status --short; git diff --stat; cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r1s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Small tagged bimodal predictor removes correctly predicted redirect bubbles\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline always fetches PC+4 and flushes the combinational fetch/decode\n  instruction whenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump. This costs one\n  bubble for each taken control transfer, including repeatedly taken loop\n  branches and calls. CoreMark executes these frequently. The measured worst\n  path runs through forwarding and the combinational M-extension ALU, so a\n  modest fetch predictor can improve iterations per cycle without extending\n  that dominant arithmetic path. Its timing and area costs must still be\n  measured rather than assuming that better prediction guarantees better fitness.\n  A retirement-trace model of the existing canonical CoreMark ELF counts\n  357,527 baseline redirect bubbles inside the markers and 73,619 successor\n  mispredictions with this 32-entry predictor. Against 4,491,485 baseline\n  cycles, that suggests a 6.75% gain if every avoided bubble saves a cycle,\n  or approximately 5.2% after estimating 22% overlap with instruction-bus\n  stalls, at unchanged Fmax. This is a sizing estimate, not an RTL or Gowin\n  measurement. A 64-entry direct-mapped table improves the same overlap\n  estimate only to 5.4%, so choose 32 entries to limit physical overhead.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one 32-entry direct-mapped, fully tagged branch-target buffer with a\n  two-bit saturating direction counter per entry. Index by fetch PC[6:2],\n  store PC[31:7] as the tag and the full aligned target PC[31:2], and reset\n  valid bits. Read it combinationally from the registered fetch PC; a valid\n  tag hit with counter[1]=1 predicts the stored target, otherwise predict\n  PC+4. Use this lookup rather than instruction predecode or an IF target\n  adder. Train conditional branches on their resolved taken/not-taken\n  outcome, allocating at weakly taken (10) or weakly not taken (01), and\n  train aligned legal JAL/JALR targets as strongly taken (11). Train only\n  once when a valid\n…[3162 of 6234 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d0f2424fb88727a2]…\nctor training information and suppress recovery\n      and training during dmem holds. Preserve resolved RVFI and link values.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire EX predictor training to IF and retain the existing hazard and\n      flush paths, now driven by prediction mismatches rather than every\n      taken transfer. Preserve all top-level ports and nret=1 retirement.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add directed predictor tests for loop warm-up and exit recovery,\n      alternating outcomes, forward taken branches, JAL links, changing\n      JALR targets, index collisions, and stale hits on replaced ordinary\n      instructions. Drive deterministic imem/dmem stalls, including an EX\n      branch held behind an older memory operation; check exactly-once\n      stores, retirement PCs/order, misaligned target traps, and flushed\n      wrong-path instructions. Check that a warmed taken loop avoids the\n      baseline redirect bubble using external cycle/retirement observations.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
