{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'predictor|hyp-20261002-003' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261002-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Small tagged bimodal predictor removes correctly predicted redirect bubbles\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches PC+4 and flushes the combinational fetch/decode\\ninstruction whenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump. This costs one\\nbubble for each taken control transfer, including repeatedly taken loop\\nbranches and calls. CoreMark executes these frequently. The measured worst\\npath runs through forwarding and the combinational M-extension ALU, so a\\nmodest fetch predictor can improve iterations per cycle without extending\\nthat dominant arithmetic path. Its timing and area costs must still be\\nmeasured rather than assuming that better prediction guarantees better fitness.\\nA retirement-trace model of the existing canonical CoreMark ELF counts\\n357,527 baseline redirect bubbles inside the markers and 73,619 successor\\nmispredictions with this 32-entry predictor. Against 4,491,485 baseline\\ncycles, that suggests a 6.75% gain if every avoided bubble saves a cycle,\\nor approximately 5.2% after estimating 22% overlap with instruction-bus\\nstalls, at unchanged Fmax. This is a sizing estimate, not an RTL or Gowin\\nmeasurement. A 64-entry direct-mapped table improves the same overlap\\nestimate only to 5.4%, so choose 32 entries to limit physical overhead.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline always fetches PC+4 and flushes the combinational fetch/decode\\ninstruction whenever EX resolves a taken branch or jump. This costs one\\nbubble for each taken control transfer, including repeatedly taken loop\\nbranches and calls. CoreMark executes these frequently. The measured worst\\npath runs through forwarding and the combinational M-extension ALU, so a\\