{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r3s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261002-003-r3s2\ntitle: \"Compact region-scoped tagged predictor preserves accuracy with narrower tables\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion combines a registered divider with a 32-entry tagged\n  bimodal predictor and reaches 113.27 fitness at 47.673 MHz and 4,208,611\n  bracketed CoreMark cycles. Prior predictor history estimates only 73,619\n  remaining successor mispredictions, so even eliminating all of them would\n  save less than 2% of runtime. Increasing predictor accuracy alone is\n  unlikely to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin. Instead, the existing table\n  spends 25 tag bits and 30 target bits per entry representing the whole\n  32-bit address space, despite the platform's 1 MiB executable RAM region.\n  A predictor that covers that region explicitly can preserve useful\n  predictions while reducing table storage, read muxes, and routing load.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the full-address 32-entry BTB with a region-scoped tagged BTB\n  covering executable addresses [0x00000000, 0x00100000). Retain the same\n  index PC[6:2], 32 valid bits, two-bit saturating direction counters,\n  allocation policy, and once-per-EX-advance training. Store only PC[19:7]\n  as the 13-bit tag and target[19:2] as the 18-bit aligned target. Fetch hit\n  requires PC[31:20] == 0 as well as a valid low-tag match; reconstruct a\n  selected target as {12'b0, target_low, 2'b00}. Only allocate entries when\n  the legal aligned source and target are both in the covered region.\n  Region eligibility is a prediction policy, never an architectural check:\n  instructions, jumps, and branch targets outside the region must continue\n  to execute and resolve using full 32-bit PCs and the existing fallback\n  and EX recovery paths. Do not truncate architectural PCs or add traps.\n\n  Separate source-tag matching from allocation eligibility. A source in\n  the covered region that matches an existing entry but resolves as a\n  non-control instruction, illegal/misaligned transfer, or transfer to an\n  uncovered target invalidates that entry. \n…[1986 of 5058 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9a72fc2501749eaf]…\nining hits on a zero\n      PC[31:20], qualify allocations on full source/target range and\n      alignment, reconstruct predicted targets with explicit zero high\n      bits, and invalidate matching sources resolving to uncovered or\n      otherwise ineligible transfers. Preserve 32 entries, counter policy,\n      reset, all ports, accepted-fetch enables, and EX recovery priority.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Retain all existing tests and add directed warm-branch/JAL/JALR\n      checks at low and upper covered addresses, same-index tag collisions,\n      stale-entry recovery, and full-width out-of-region fallback. Check\n      that high-PC aliases cannot select or invalidate low-PC entries and\n      that an in-region JALR changing to an uncovered target recovers using\n      the actual full-width target. Combine these cases with deterministic\n      imem/dmem stalls and division holds; verify retirement PCs/order,\n      links, traps, exactly-once stores, and warmed transfer bubble removal.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
