{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'] in ['hyp-20261002-003-r4s2','hyp-20261002-003-r5s2']:\n  print(d['id']); print(d['hypothesis']['motivation']); print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261002-003-r4s2\nThe current champion already has a tagged bimodal predictor and registered\nMUL/JALR completion, but fetch still stops on every load-use, data-memory,\nand execute-busy hold. This wastes ready instruction-bus opportunities and\nleaves later instruction stalls uncovered. Improving direction accuracy\nalone is unlikely to exceed the 4.6% acceptance margin: the earlier trace\nestimate found only 73,619 remaining recoveries among roughly 4.2 million\ncycles. The previous four-entry predicted-fetch queue reduced its parent's\nmeasured cycles from 4,208,611 to 3,983,322, a 5.35% reduction, and improved\nthat parent's fitness by 6.91%. It lost the tournament to registered\nMUL/JALR, rather than demonstrating that buffering cannot improve that\nnewer core. Apply the frontend change to the present 58.072 MHz,\n4,409,538-cycle champion, preserving its registered arithmetic and\nindirect-target timing boundaries. The additional execute holds provide\nfurther opportunities to fetch ahead. An isolated four-entry probe on\nthis exact base now measures 4,053,425 bracketed cycles and 57.275 MHz\nmedian Gowin Fmax, or 141.30 fitness, 7.29% above the champion. A fresh\nbaseline run reproduces 4,409,538 cycles with the same canonical ELF.\n\nDecouple the existing predicted fetch stream from backend acceptance with\none compact four-entry flow-through instruction queue. Keep the current\n32-entry tagged BTB, two-bit counters, EX recovery, and registered\nmultiplier/divider/JALR completion intact. This is a single frontend\nhypothesis: use already predicted successors to collect accepted bus\ninstructions during backend holds, then supply them during instruction\nstalls. Do not combine it with a new direction predictor or a cache.\n\nSeparate bus acceptance from ID acceptance. A nonempty queue presents\nits oldest instruction independently of current imemReady. An empty\nqueue bypasses the raw bus instruction and lookup metadata directly to\nID, retaining the champion's zero-wait latency. Accept an instruction\nfrom the bus only with imemRea\n…[10081 of 13153 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:092413c86e697b80]…\n million. Measure\nactual canonical stalled CoreMark cycles and median Gowin Fmax;\nstorage and head mux overhead can still erase the benefit. Do not\ninfer acceptance from the prior flow-through queue's cycle count.\n\nThe bounded prototype's placement options [0, 1, 2] measure\n[61.323, 63.982, 63.982] MHz, with 4,236 LUT4, 1,164 FF, 76 LUT-RAM,\nsix BSRAMs and two DSPs. The unchanged CoreMark harness reports all\ncanonical CRCs, both timing markers, the required validation UART\nliteral, successful completion, and no OOB. Full RVFI ISS comparison\npasses selftest and all three random programs with zero waits and\nwith both random bus stalls, plus a directed JAL at 0x000ffffc that\nmust turn around without speculative access to 0x00100000: ten runs\ntotal. The probe is under cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/\nregistered4_frontier and leaves champion RTL unchanged. Reimplement\nfrom this hypothesis and run all unchanged correctness/fitness gates;\nfull formal and directed FIFO/reset/stall validation remain required.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
