{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_68","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -u - <<'PY' > cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/registered_small.log 2>&1\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json,subprocess,os\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build,summarize\nfrom tools.eval.fpga import validate_coremark_uart\nroot=Path('.').resolve(); probe=root/'cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe'\ndef one(depth):\n b=probe/f'r8_registered{depth}'\n env=dict(os.environ,RTL_DIR=str(b/'rtl'),OBJ_DIR=str(b/'obj_dir'),NRET='1')\n with (b/'build.log').open('w') as f: subprocess.run(['bash','test/cosim/build.sh'],env=env,stdout=f,stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,check=True)\n p=subprocess.run([str(b/'obj_dir/cosim_sim'),str(probe/'r8_fifo/coremark.elf'),'50000000','--bench','--istall','--dstall'],capture_output=True,text=True,timeout=120)\n (b/'coremark.out').write_text(p.stdout)\n r=[json.loads(s) for s in p.stdout.splitlines() if s.strip()][-1]\n c=r['bench_stop_cycle']-r['bench_start_cycle']; v=validate_coremark_uart(r['uart'],10)\n s=dict(returncode=p.returncode,cycles=c,completed=r['ebreak'],oob=r['oob'],bracketed=r['bench_bracketed'],crc=v,cycle_savings_pct=100*(1-c/4483027))\n (b/'sim.json').write_text(json.dumps(s,indent=2)); print(depth,'sim',json.dumps(s),flush=True)\n with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as pool:\n  fs=[pool.submit(build,root,b/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',i,b/f'gowin_p{i}') for i in (0,1,2)]\n  result=[f.result() for f in fs]\n t=summarize(result); (b/'timing.json').write_text(json.dumps(t,indent=2))\n fitness=1e7*t['fmax_mhz']/c\n print(depth,'timing',json.dumps(t),'fitness',fitness,'delta',100*(fitness/187.23-1),flush=True)\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as pool: list(pool.map(one,[2,3]))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r8s2.yaml')\nh=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text())\nh['title']='Two-entry fixed-head predicted fetch queue preserves operand timing'\nh['motivation']='''The champion scores 187.23 iterations/sec at 83.934 MHz and 4,483,027 bracketed CoreMark cycles. The existing 32-entry tagged BTB already handles most transfer bubbles, but fetch loses ready bus opportunities during load-use, data-memory and blocking-execute holds. Prior queues saved cycles while exposing frontend or operand-delivery timing. Complete execution operands and MEM recovery are now registered. A small queue with a constant flop head can overlap predicted fetch with these holds without an indexed queue read or live instruction bypass feeding decode. Exploratory four-entry circular and flow-through probes saved 7.82% of cycles but reached only 62.122 and 70.220 MHz; a four-entry registered fixed head saved 5.99% but reached 78.500 MHz. Reduce the same predictor lookahead mechanism to two entries to limit area and routing pressure while preserving its timing boundaries.'''\nh['hypothesis']='''Add one two-entry registered queue of complete predicted-fetch snapshots inside if_stage. This is a single predicted-fetch lookahead change: use the existing BTB to accept bus words during backend holds and supply buffered words during instruction stalls. Keep BTB size, full tags and targets, counter policy, MEM-accepted training and recovery, complete operand capture, producer forwarding and all arithmetic latencies unchanged.\n\nUse two separately generated constant-index flop words, with the oldest snapshot always in entry zero and the second in entry one. Store full PC, instruction, prediction_selected, full predicted_target and a one-bit frontier marker. Do not use dynamically indexed storage or read pointers and do not bypass a live bus instruction into decode. Only the previously registered fixed head feeds instruction, PC and prediction payloads. Occupancy controls head validity separately from these data bits. ID reads live register values on consumption. Each accepted bus word enters the queue once; PCs are stored directly, never reconstructed from consumed predictions.\n\nMaintain occupancy from zero to two. Accept a bus word only with imemReady, capacity at cycle start and no reset, recovery or frontier wait. Ordinary fetch-PC enable depends on registered capacity and bus readiness, never a backend pop. Deliberately refuse same-cycle replacement of a full queue. Advance fetch PC using the prediction for the accepted word only. Pop only a valid registered head that ID can consume without load-use, genuine older dmem wait or execute_busy. On pop shift entry one to entry zero. Append an accepted bus snapshot at old occupancy minus pop, giving append priority over shift for that destination. Use constant-index per-word write enables comparing occupancy minus pop with the bank index. Push alone increments count, pop alone decrements count and simultaneous push/pop preserves count. Invalid words need no payload reset; reset clears occupancy. An empty queue produces an ID bubble and an accepted bus word becomes available on the following cycle. Accept the deliberate refill cycle to keep queue availability out of register-address and operand-data selection.\n\nHandle raw imemReady locally in IF. Remove it from the hazard unit's backend-consumption hold; load-use detection inspects the presented head and its availability. Keep raw head payload independent of redirect. The existing ID flush cancels younger capture and overrides holds; gate pop, push and head validity on accepted recovery. Preserve EX/MEM genuine memory waits, retained MEM/WB forwarding data, held operand refresh and pending-operation cancellation. Do not add queue controls to registered operand outputs, ALU data or registered resolution.\n\nA MEM-accepted redirect overrides every hold, clears queue occupancy, discards same-edge fetch acceptance and installs the full resolved target. Correct predictions preserve queued work. Train on the existing once-per-MEM-acceptance event, including its redirect edge, never on queue activity or squashed work. Carry each snapshot's original prediction through ID and blocking-operation capture. Preserve recovery for direction and target disagreement, stale ordinary or illegal predictions and misaligned transfers.\n\nBound speculative runahead at the executable-memory frontier. When an accepted word predicts a successor outside [0, 0x00100000), save the full successor, mark that exact snapshot, and hold physical fetch PC at its accepted address. While waiting accept no duplicate bus word; queued work drains. Carry the marker through ID/EX, EX/MEM and pending-operation payloads. The existing train_valid MEM acceptance carrying the marker validates that exact dynamic instruction. If no recovery is required, release the saved full successor as architectural demand. Recovery and reset cancel the wait; an older recovery squashes its marked younger instruction. Only one marker can be outstanding. Do not authorize release by PC equality, truncate high address bits, clamp a true architectural demand or block symbolic forward progress.\n\nKeep nret=1, all top-level ports, full PC/target/address widths, validated default-illegal decoding, actual RVFI operands and successors, monotonic contiguous retirement order, exactly-once stores, real and ALTOPS arithmetic and all unchanged evaluation gates. Use packed vector wires for inter-bank snapshot connections and whole-word flop writes for formal frontend compatibility. The added frontier bit changes ID/EX to 329 bits and EX/MEM to 296 bits; update every explicit packed mask and test layout consistently.\n\nMeasure canonical bracketed CoreMark cycles and median vendor Fmax together. Four-entry measurements establish stall-overlap potential, not acceptance of this two-entry proposal. Small fixed-head storage is intended to retain about 5-6% cycle savings while keeping median Fmax near the champion. Reimplement and rerun unchanged correctness and fitness gates; scratch artifacts are transient and deleted before evaluation. Preserve exact architectural test assertions. For tests that change imem contents, schedule replacement before the affected word's bus acceptance or explicitly check the saved accepted snapshot; replacement must not retroactively rewrite queued instructions. Update cycle expectations only for the declared registered refill behavior, retaining exact instructions, operands, traps, successors, order and side effects.'''\nh['expected_impact']={'fitness_delta_pct':6,'confidence':'medium'}\nh['changes'][0]['description']='''Implement the inline two-entry queue with separately generated constant-index flop words and fixed entry-zero head, occupancy-only validity, no live bypass, shift/append updates, independent conservative bus acceptance, redirect/reset flush and exact marked frontier validation. Preserve indexed tagged BTB policy and MEM learning latency.'''\nh['changes'][-1]['description']='''Retain all architectural assertions and add registered refill, buffered delivery under imem stalls, both occupancies, simultaneous shift/append, load-use and older dmem/execute holds, nonempty recovery, reset, stale predictions and frontier turnaround. Cover exact dynamic frontier marking with repeated PCs, and require correct RVFI/order and exactly-once stores. Adapt mutation triggers to accepted snapshots and explicitly check the declared refill timing.'''\np.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(h,sort_keys=False,width=90))\njsonschema.validate(h,json.load(open('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json')))\nprint('Revised complete schema-valid hypothesis:',p)\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
