20261002-003-r3s2 (regression, +6.91%): A compact four-entry flow-through fetch FIFO overlaps load-use/dmem/divider holds with instruction-bus acceptance, but its gain lost to registering MUL/JALR (+16.27%), showi\n…[1920 of 4992 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6b32009aa5726edb]…\n4.74%), so predictor maintenance and operand delivery remain competing timing targets.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-002-r7s1 (improvement, +11.64%): Registering complete ID/EX operands and EX/MEM recovery together isolates EX from incoming bypass selection and outgoing frontend feedback, while prediction limits the extra recovery bubble to misses so Fmax gains outweigh CoreMark's cycle penalty.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-003-r7s2 (regression, -13.12%): Replacing indexed BTB muxes with parallel full-tag flop banks and local hysteresis exposed a fetch-PC-to-ID prediction-mismatch critical path, lowering median Fmax to 64.252 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles; balanced lookup trees must be timed together with decode's target qualification.\n- 2026-10-02 hyp-20261002-001-r8s0 (regression, +4.45%): Removing EX's outer in.valid payload mask eliminates duplicate gating before fast_emit without changing instruction latency, but this local simplification yielded only 4.45% more throughput, below the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
