{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_69","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build_all,summarize\nimport json\nbase=Path('cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/queue3_valids')\nr=build_all(Path('.'),base/'rtl','fpga/core_bench_si.sv',base/'timing')\n(base/'timing.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,indent=2)); print(json.dumps(summarize(r),indent=2))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"placement_failed\": false,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 76.834,\n  \"seeds\": [\n    77.961,\n    76.834,\n    76.834\n  ],\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      77.961,\n      8\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      76.834,\n      9\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      76.834,\n      9\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lut4\": 6576,\n  \"ff\": 2543,\n  \"lutram\": 44,\n  \"bsram\": 4,\n  \"dsp\": 2,\n  \"logic_levels\": 8,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.alu_b_val_3_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -7.827,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 18.125,\n    \"logic_cells\": 8,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_mem\",\n      \"cpu/u_id\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_72", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261002-003-r9s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261002-003-r9s2\ntitle: \\\"Three-slot predicted fetch queue with local occupancy control\\\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The two-entry fixed-head predicted fetch queue reached 195.67 iterations/sec,\n  a 4.51 percent gain that narrowly missed the 4.6 percent acceptance margin.\n  A three-entry prototype saves 5.13 percent of canonical CoreMark cycles,\n  but binary occupancy and subtract-to-select append control reached only\n  78.578 MHz. Preserve the additional predicted lookahead while replacing\n  global occupancy arithmetic with local occupied bits and constant-bank\n  append enables. Execution, forwarding and the existing BTB policy stay\n  unchanged; this is one predictor-front-end architectural change.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add three registered complete predicted-fetch snapshots inside if_stage.\n  Keep the existing 32-entry fully tagged bimodal BTB, full targets, exact\n  counter policy and once-per-MEM-acceptance training latency. Accept bus\n  words during backend holds and deliver saved words during imem stalls.\n  Use separately generated constant-index flop words, with entry zero\n  always the oldest snapshot and the sole source of decode data. Store full\n  PC, instruction, prediction_selected, full predicted_target and a frontier\n  marker. Use packed-vector inter-bank connections and whole-word writes\n  for formal compatibility. Never bypass live instruction data into decode\n  or reconstruct queued PCs. Resolve operands when ID actually consumes.\n\n  Replace binary count/pointers with registered prefix occupancy v0,v1,v2:\n  empty=000, one=001, two=011, full=111. Reset/recovery clear the bits.\n  pop = v0 AND backend-ready AND not reset/recovery.\n  push = imemReady AND \"'!v2 AND !frontier-wait AND not reset/recovery.\n  Refuse same-cycle replacement of an initially full queue, keeping push\n  and ordinary fetch-PC advancement independent of pop/backend-ready.\n  Derive the following constant-bank append enables from OLD state:\n  append0 = push AND (!v0 OR (pop AND !v1));\n  append1 = push AND ((v0 AND !v1 AND !pop) OR (v1 AND !v2 AND pop));\n  append2 = push AND v1 AND !pop.\n  Append has priority over shift for its bank. Otherwise pop shifts bank\n  one into zero and bank two into one. Bank two holds unless appended.\n  Invalid payloads need no reset. Update occupancy using OLD state:\n  v0_next = push OR (v0 AND !pop) OR v1;\n  v1_next = (v1 AND !pop) OR v2 OR (push AND v0 AND (!pop OR v1));\n  v2_next = (v2 AND !pop) OR (push AND v1 AND !pop).\n  This preserves the three-entry prototype'\"'s schedule while removing\n  decrement-to-append logic and indexed queue reads. Test all transitions.\n\n  Handle raw imemReady locally in IF. Backend consumption uses head\n  availability, head source fields, load-use, genuine older dmem hold and\n  execute_busy; raw bus readiness must not hold an available head. Keep\n  payload bits independent of redirect and qualify validity separately.\n  ID flush overrides holds. MEM-accepted recovery cancels push/pop, clears\n  occupancy and installs the full resolved target regardless of fetch or\n  backend holds. Correct predictions retain buffered work. Advance fetch\n  PC only on actual push and keep each accepted word's exact prediction\n  snapshot through ID and pending operations. Learn only on the existing\n  once-per-MEM-acceptance event, including its own redirect edge; queue\n  activity and squashed work cannot train independently.\n\n  Bound speculation at the executable-memory frontier. If a pushed word's\n  predicted successor is outside [0,0x00100000), mark that exact dynamic\n  snapshot, save its full successor and hold physical fetch PC at its\n  accepted address. While waiting accept no duplicates; queued work drains.\n  Carry the marker through ID/EX, EX/MEM and pending payloads. Its exact\n  MEM-accepted event authorizes release of the saved full successor as\n  architectural demand when no recovery is required. Recovery/reset cancel\n  the wait. Never release by PC equality, truncate high bits, clamp actual\n  demand or block symbolic forward progress. Only one marker is outstanding.\n\n  Preserve nret=1, external IO, full PCs/targets, default-illegal validated\n  decoding, all traps, actual RVFI operands/successors, contiguous order,\n  held operand refresh, load-use/bus semantics, exactly-once stores and\n  real/ALTOPS arithmetic. The marker makes IF/ID 99, ID/EX 329 and EX/MEM\n  296 bits; update packed masks/test layouts consistently. Use numeric or\n  frontend-supported mask widths: Yosys rejects \"'$bits(type) inside the\n  exploratory replicated-mask expression. Change no evaluation contracts.\n\n  The binary-control three-entry prototype passes full RVFI ISS cosim for\n  selftest and all three random programs, both zero-wait and stalled. Its\n  canonical CoreMark run validates every CRC, UART success text, timing\n  markers, completion and no OOB, taking 4,252,977 bracketed cycles versus\n  the freshly reproduced champion'\"'s 4,483,027. These establish the lookahead\n  opportunity, not final acceptance. At that schedule median Gowin Fmax\n  must exceed approximately 83.25 MHz to beat 187.23 by more than 4.6\n  percent. Rerun all gates on the final local-occupancy version; a competing\n  operand-forwarding timing path is the main risk. Prototype sources under\n  cores/bench/generated/hypothesis_probe/queue3_valids are transient and\n  must be reimplemented because generated artifacts are removed before eval.\n\n  Retain all existing architectural assertions. Registered refill adds one\n  cycle after recovery. Mutation tests must change a word before its bus\n  acceptance or explicitly verify the previously accepted snapshot before\n  the replacement. Internal checks must use the presented queue head,\n  rather than assuming io_imemAddr is decode PC. Adapt only declared\n  refill timing and fetch-position assumptions while keeping exact PC\n  sequences, operands, traps, successors, retirement order and side effects.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement three fixed flop snapshots with prefix occupancy, direct\n      constant-bank append enables, conservative independent push, shift\n      and pop. Preserve BTB policy, registered-head-only delivery, exact\n      frontier marking/release, and accepted-recovery flush.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Consume queued instructions independently of raw imemReady while\n      preserving head-valid-qualified load-use, execute_busy, genuine\n      older memory holds and existing flush/hold priorities.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire frontend-local bus acceptance, backend consumption controls and\n      the MEM event's frontier marker. Preserve operand/producer wiring,\n      accepted recovery and all nret=1 external ports.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add one frontier marker to IF/ID, ID/EX and EX/MEM; preserve full\n      PCs and prediction snapshots and all downstream RVFI semantics.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture, hold and flush the marker with its instruction. Preserve\n      complete operand capture and held-operand refresh unchanged.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Copy the marker into fast and pending payloads and expose it on MEM\n      acceptance. Update EX/MEM masks to 296 bits, preserving arithmetic,\n      completion, producer forwarding, learning and registered recovery.\n  - file: test/test_ex_stage.py\n    description: |\n      Update explicit packed layouts to ID/EX 329 and EX/MEM 296 bits.\n      Preserve exact checks and test marker hold/completion/reset/squash.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check every prefix occupancy and push/pop transition, registered\n      refill, buffered delivery under imem stalls, backend holds, recovery\n      and exact dynamic frontier validation. Adapt mutation timing to bus\n      acceptance and internal probes to the presented head; retain exact\n      architectural assertions and require unchanged formal, stalled ISS\n      cosim and canonical CoreMark CRC/marker/UART/OOB gates.\nYAML\n\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
